A||N||4||MrFeeArrow||Engine stumbles under load||||1||
Z||000000||MrFeeArrow||07-24-2001||07:28 PM||||1985 2M4 has a stumble on problem when engine is under load only. <P>Anyone know where to start?||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||07-24-2001||07:37 PM||||sounds like your secondary ignition is breaking down, ie. spark plugs, wires, dist cap, rotor.  most likely plug wires.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrFeeArrow||07-24-2001||08:03 PM||||Thanks PBJ,...This is an area I've had trouble with about every 18 to 24 months. I change the plugs every year and the cap/rotor/button and wires every other. This just seems be a re-occurring problem with this vehicle. I did notice that the cap doesn't have a rubber grommet to seal it against the distributor,...is there such a thing?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-25-2001||12:00 AM||||check base timing and replace MAP sensor with ACdelco or Borg Warner brand part. A marginal MAP combined with off timing or marginal ignition will play hell with things. MAP is used to calculate "vacuum advance" amongst other things. MAP must be connected to the right port on the TBI or it won't read correctly. <P>make sure all vac lines are good and connected correctly. It matters where they are connected.<P>Get a set of decent wires. See if  <A HREF="http://www.magnecor.com/" TARGET=_blank>Magnecor</A> has them for the distributor motor. (Actually they have wires for anything... they will make them on demand or sell you the stuff to make you own.) These will likely be the last wires you buy fo it. The 7mm or 8mm magnecor will work on it and fit the OE wire guides. (I've used them before. I'll be putting them on my DIS motor in the near future.)<P>Most dist cap are unsealed. You need a certain amount of fresh air in them. If yours is getting water in it then try a very thin bead of RTV silicone along the top 2/3 of the rim. DO NOT seal the whole rim. If the dist is collecting water from deep pudles or condensation then drill a very small drain hole down low on the dist. make sure you don't hit anything you shouldn't with the drill. You want anything that collects/condenses to drain easily from it. (Small as in 1/16 to 3/32 inch.)<P>If you are running an aftermarket ignition that could also be part of your problem. After market ignitions are known for sortening the life of caps and rotors on some cars. Especially if you don't buy the best ones to start with. I'd use ACdelco or Borg Warner for these. Maybe Standard. not anyone else.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrFeeArrow||07-25-2001||08:16 PM||||Thanks for the help Ogre,... I replaced the Cap/Rotor and wires today with NAPA's Echlend brand products as you sugested, My little 4 banger is bangin' or should I say purrrrrrring!! My dad gave this car and I just can't part with it, I just want it to live forever......... ||||reg||10
